# Cairn v2 ingest server flags. Install to /etc/cairn/server.env (root:cairn 0640).
#
# systemd word-splits a bare $CAIRN_ARGS, so the whole flag list fits in one
# variable. Keep it on a single line; a trailing backslash is not continued here
# the way a shell would continue it.

# ─── mTLS, the intended configuration ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The device verifies the server against a CA pinned in firmware, and presents
# its own certificate whose CommonName must equal its 32-hex device id. The
# server compares that name to the device id inside the signed manifest and
# refuses a mismatch, so a stolen card cannot upload as a different device.
#
# This is why the ingest listener is not behind a reverse proxy. The handler
# reads r.TLS.PeerCertificates to do that comparison; terminating TLS upstream
# makes r.TLS nil and reduces the check to trusting a forwarded header, which
# anything able to reach the backend could forge.

CAIRN_ARGS=-addr :8443 -data /var/lib/cairn -tls-cert /etc/cairn/certs/server.pem -tls-key /etc/cairn/certs/server-key.pem -tls-client-ca /etc/cairn/certs/ca.pem

# ─── plaintext, for a device that cannot do mTLS yet ─────────────────────────
#
# A device needs two things before it can speak mTLS, and both require physical
# access: CAIRN_SERVER_CA_PEM compiled into firmware, and client.crt/client.key
# placed in /cairn/certs/ on its card. Until then it talks plain HTTP on :8080
# and this is the only configuration it can reach.
#
# Not as bad as it sounds, and not an excuse either: a receipt's Ed25519
# signature is what authorizes deleting data, not the transport, so plaintext
# changes who can read an upload rather than whether a deletion is legitimate.
#
# CAIRN_ARGS=-dev -addr :8080 -data /var/lib/cairn

# ─── OTA, optional ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Append -firmware-dir /var/lib/cairn/firmware to serve signed update
# descriptors. Omitting it disables the OTA endpoints entirely rather than
# serving an empty list, so a misconfigured path cannot look like "no update".
